    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nAn LSP server that registers `workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles` watchers with a `globPattern` that `vim.glob.to_lpeg` cannot parse hits the `assert` in `runtime/lua/vim/glob.lua:373`, which propagates and aborts the **entire** `client/registerCapability` request. No registration in that batch takes effect, and the user sees:\n\n```\nLSP[tsgo]: Error SERVER_REQUEST_HANDLER_ERROR: \".../share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/glob.lua:373: Invalid glob: bundled:///libs/**/*\"\n```\n\nRepro (no LSP server needed):\n\n```\nnvim --headless +'lua vim.glob.to_lpeg(\"bundled:///libs/**/*\")' +qa\n```\n\n```\nE5108: Lua: .../share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/glob.lua:373: Invalid glob: bundled:///libs/**/*\nstack traceback:\n\t[C]: in function 'assert'\n\t.../share/nvim/runtime/lua/vim/glob.lua:373: in function 'to_lpeg'\n\t...\n```\n\n### Why this happens with tsgo\n\n`tsgo` (`@typescript/native-preview`) registers file watchers over its shipped lib files using a `bundled://` URI scheme, e.g. `bundled:///libs/**/*`. A client cannot file-watch a virtual URI, so these watchers are unusable anyway — yet today they take down the whole registration batch.\n\nThe unguarded `assert` is in `runtime/lua/vim/lsp/_watchfiles.lua` `register()` (around L64-83), where `glob.to_lpeg(glob_pattern)` is called for each watcher with no `pcall`. One bad pattern `error()`s out of the request handler.\n\n### Related\n\nSame crash site as:\n\n- #37204 — `dartls` with `**/**.dart` (globstar expansion; `bug-regression`, `has:bisected`, open)\n- #37238 — PowerShell Editor Services (closed)\n\nThose are different root causes (globstar grammar / Windows path), but all three would be fixed by making the watchfiles path defensive instead of asserting on the first unparseable pattern.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nUnparseable/unsupported watchers should be skipped (ideally logged once); remaining registrations in the same `client/registerCapability` batch should still apply. A single bad `globPattern` from one server should not nuke capability registration for that server (or, in pathological cases, for co-batched registrations).\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nIn `runtime/lua/vim/lsp/_watchfiles.lua` `register()` (~L64-83), wrap each `glob.to_lpeg(glob_pattern)` in `pcall` and `continue` the loop on failure (log once) instead of `error()`-ing. A similar guard may be warranted in `client.lua:_get_registrations` (~L1044) where the same `to_lpeg` call appears.\n\n### Environment\n\n- nvim v0.12.3 (Homebrew, stable)\n- tsgo via `@typescript/native-preview` 7.0.0-dev\n- macOS 15\n",
